<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Samanid Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">819 CE – 999 CE · capital Bukhara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Persian dynasty ruling from Bukhara that revived New Persian as a literary language and made Transoxiana a centre of Islamic learning.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Samanids descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saman-khuda">Saman Khuda</a>, a Persian landowner near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. In 819 the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> gave his four grandsons governorships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Ferghana</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-chach">Chach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasr-i-samanid">Nasr ibn Ahmad</a> unified their holdings, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad">Isma'il ibn Ahmad</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> in 900, ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a> as Abbasid governors.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> ran a sophisticated bureaucracy of divans modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, with powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> such as al-Bal'ami and al-Jayhani. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a> from Samanid mints flowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> trade in slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. The court patronised the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudaki">Rudaki</a> and commissioned Persian translations, while scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Ibn Sina</a> and al-Razi worked under their rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> spread to the Turkic steppe.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Turkic slave generals gained power in the 10th century, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khurasan</a>. In 999 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and ended the dynasty. The Samanids are remembered in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> as founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> statehood.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/samanid-empire.jpg" alt="Samanid Empire" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: Original file by Ro4444, edited by me · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Samanid_amirate_at_the_death_of_Nasr_II,_943.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>819 CE – 999 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bukhara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nasr I, Isma'il ibn Ahmad, Nasr II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, <a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Samanids, Samanid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=909&amp;focus=samanid-empire" aria-label="Samanid Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6268 754 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Samanid Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6588 1010L6480 949M6588 1010L6696 1010M6588 1010L6749 974M6588 1010L6134 1224M6588 1010L6820 915M6588 1010L6748 1217M6588 1010L6600 1305M6588 1010L6316 1118M6588 1010L6326 1247M6588 1010L6470 1341"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6480 949h0M6696 1010h0M6749 974h0M6134 1224h0M6820 915h0M6748 1217h0M6600 1305h0M6316 1118h0M6326 1247h0M6470 1341h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6588 1010h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6588 1010h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bukhara. <a href="/atlas?year=909&amp;focus=samanid-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
